<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE policyconfig PUBLIC
 "-//freedesktop//DTD PolicyKit Policy Configuration 1.0//EN"
 "http://www.freedesktop.org/standards/PolicyKit/1/policyconfig.dtd">

<!--
  Three actions, not one and not twenty-three.

  One would have made every prompt say the same thing, and the prompt is the
  only part of this file a user ever reads. Twenty-three — one per mutating
  IPC method — would be a second copy of the method table, free to drift from
  the first, and nobody would ever write a rule against `share.make_writable`
  that they would not write against `share.add`.

  The line that earns a separate action is *configuring the machine* against
  *using what is already configured*. Everything in the first two actions
  outlives the session and applies to every user on the machine; the third
  does not.
-->
<policyconfig>
  <vendor>SMBPal</vendor>
  <vendor_url>https://github.com/smbpal/smbpal-desktop</vendor_url>
  <icon_name>smbpal</icon_name>

  <action id="org.smbpal.manage-shares">
    <description>Change the folders this computer shares</description>
    <message>Authentication is required to change the folders this computer shares on the network</message>
    <defaults>
      <allow_any>auth_admin</allow_any>
      <allow_inactive>auth_admin</allow_inactive>
      <!--
        _keep, and only here. Adding a share is several calls that look like
        one action to the person making it: add, set a password, apply. Three
        prompts for one intention is how people learn to type their password
        without reading the box.

        What _keep actually retains, established on hardware 30 August 2026:
        polkit keys a temporary authorization to the *subject*, and the subject
        the daemon supplies is a unix-process. So it is kept for as long as the
        process that earned it lives, and a different process never inherits it.

        That divides the two clients, and in the right direction. The GUI is one
        long-lived process, so its add / set password / apply carry one subject
        and prompt once — verified. The CLI is a new process per command, so
        every command authenticates, and no amount of _keep will change that:
        each `smbpal` invocation is a new subject by construction. Which is the
        correct outcome anyway. One command is one act, and the multi-prompt
        problem this exists to solve was always the window, never the shell.

        A `unix-session` subject would keep across CLI invocations and is not
        reachable: `pkcheck` can express --process or --system-bus-name and
        nothing else, so it would mean the D-Bus API and python3-gi inside the
        root daemon. Not worth a dependency to remove a prompt from the one
        path where prompting is right.
      -->
      <allow_active>auth_admin_keep</allow_active>
    </defaults>
  </action>

  <action id="org.smbpal.manage-connections">
    <description>Change the network shares this computer connects to</description>
    <message>Authentication is required to change the network shares this computer connects to</message>
    <defaults>
      <allow_any>auth_admin</allow_any>
      <allow_inactive>auth_admin</allow_inactive>
      <allow_active>auth_admin_keep</allow_active>
    </defaults>
  </action>

  <action id="org.smbpal.use-connections">
    <description>Connect to and disconnect from a configured network share</description>
    <message>Authentication is required to connect to a network share</message>
    <defaults>
      <allow_any>auth_admin</allow_any>
      <allow_inactive>auth_admin</allow_inactive>
      <!--
        `yes`, deliberately, and it is the one value in this file that needs
        defending.

        A configured connection is an armed systemd automount. Any local user
        who so much as runs `ls` on the mountpoint mounts it, with no prompt,
        because that is what an automount is for and §3h chose it on purpose.
        Putting a password prompt on the button that does the same thing would
        protect nothing and teach the user that SMBPal's prompts are noise —
        which is the last thing to teach someone whose next prompt guards
        changing what the machine shares with the network.

        Disconnecting is a nuisance one logged-in user can inflict on another.
        On the single-user machine §5 describes that is not worth a prompt.
        It is the first thing to revisit if SMBPal ever targets a shared one.
      -->
      <allow_active>yes</allow_active>
    </defaults>
  </action>
</policyconfig>
